{"name":"Giant Onion","hex":"#665d9e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"giant","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mythical human or humanoid of very great size.","Specifically:\nAny of the gigantes, the race of giants in the Greek mythology.\nAn eoten or jotun."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Very large."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/giant"},{"term":"onion","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A monocotyledonous plant (Allium cepa), allied to garlic, used as vegetable and spice.","The bulb of such a plant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/onion"}],"entries":[{"title":"Allium giganteum","blocks":[{"text":"Allium giganteum, common name giant onion or giant leek, is an Asian species of onion in the amaryllis family (Amaryllidaceae), subfamily Allioideae, native to central and southwestern Asia but cultivated in many countries as a flowering garden plant. It is the tallest species of Allium in common cultivation, growing to 1.5 metres (4.9 ft)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/PurpleBallFlower.JPG/330px-PurpleBallFlower.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allium_giganteum","wikidata":"Q736155"},{"title":"Giant","blocks":[{"text":"In folklore, giants are beings of humanoid appearance, but are at times prodigious in size and strength or bear an otherwise notable appearance. The word giant is first attested in 1297 from Robert of Gloucester's chronicle. It is derived from the Gigantes of Greek mythology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Rhinegold_and_the_Valkyries_p_032.jpg/330px-Rhinegold_and_the_Valkyries_p_032.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giant","wikidata":"Q3707571"},{"title":"Onion","blocks":[{"text":"The onion, also known as the bulb onion or common onion, is a vegetable that is the most widely cultivated species of the genus Allium. The shallot is a botanical variety of the onion which was classified as a separate species until 2011. The onion's close relatives include garlic, scallion, leek, and chives."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Mixed_onions.jpg/330px-Mixed_onions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Onion","wikidata":"Q23485"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Allium_Giganteum_%281%29.jpg/120px-Allium_Giganteum_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Allium_Giganteum_%281%29.jpg/250px-Allium_Giganteum_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Allium_Giganteum_%281%29.jpg/120px-Allium_Giganteum_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAllium_Giganteum_(1).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Giganti_di_Messina_%28Mata_e_Grifone%29_-_Messina_%28Sicily%29_-_Italy_-_15_Aug._2009_-_%284%29.jpg/500px-Giganti_di_Messina_%28Mata_e_Grifone%29_-_Messina_%28Sicily%29_-_Italy_-_15_Aug._2009_-_%284%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Giganti_di_Messina_%28Mata_e_Grifone%29_-_Messina_%28Sicily%29_-_Italy_-_15_Aug._2009_-_%284%29.jpg/1280px-Giganti_di_Messina_%28Mata_e_Grifone%29_-_Messina_%28Sicily%29_-_Italy_-_15_Aug._2009_-_%284%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Giganti_di_Messina_%28Mata_e_Grifone%29_-_Messina_%28Sicily